everyone this is kevin from  thechestwebsite.com and today we're  going to be going over a really easy  trap in the perk defense that's going to  allow you to get some easy  easy wins so perk defense usually starts  out white playing e4 and then black  playing something like d6 and then d4  trying to control the center of the  board followed by knight to f6  knight to c3 and then pawn g6 preparing  to get fion kiddo the bishop here to g7  and you can see there's a lot of pieces  trying to defend and attack this e5  central square now usually white's going  to play either f4  you can see that's attacking this e5  square or even f3 both of those are  attacking the e5 square i think a really  fun way to play is bring out the dark  square bishop here to f4 and that's  where our trap is going to start from  adding another piece  to this e5 square but also attacking the  entire dark square diagonal here black  usually for all of those variations we  looked at usually plays bishop to g7  counter attack the dark squares in the  center of the board and then white's  going to be playing pawn to e5 saying  yes you your fiance your bishop to  castle on the king side get some safety  before you have time to do that we want  to attack right away  now the best move from black in this  position is to play knight to h5  attacking the bishop on f4 since it's  under attack this gets the knight out of  harm's way and attacks the piece here on  f4  but also pretty common for them just to  take on e5 i think you're gonna see that  more often than not and after the pawn  takes then queen comes down exchanges on  a d1 and after rook to d1 this is where  we start to see the trap unfold  because where does black move from here  one of the most common things that  you'll see from black is to play the  move knight to d7 and we'll look at what  happens if they play something else  maybe like knight two g4 as well but you  can see there's rook on d1 controlling  this d file so knight to d7  makes a lot of sense the knight center  attack needs to move that and so this  looks like it's blocking off some of the  threats that white has  with the rook here on at d1 but this  loses to the move knight to d5 and this  is what we're going to be talking about  in the video because they have fallen  for the trap and it's pretty basic moves  from black they didn't have to do  anything crazy  and even if they don't fall for it  white's going to have a pretty good and  solid position still controlling the  center of the board so after knight to  d5 really the threat is the knight  coming here to  c7 now one of the ways that black would  usually hold on to this pawn on c7 is to  play the move knight to a6  but they can't do that because we can  just take with our bishop here on a six  and after they capture we're going to  play knight c7 and this is forking the  rook here on a8 now if we come back this  is the reason we play knight d5 instead  of knight to b5 if we play knight to b5  that's not actually going to work  because they can play knight a6  defending this pawn and now our bishop  here on f1 cannot take that knight on a6  and so this would be a poor move from  white so instead we do play the correct  move because we watched the video knight  d5 attacking the pawn  and so now how does black respond from  here  they have some huge mistakes they can  make you know knight takes here on  e5 just completely ignoring the threats  here this is even worse than something  else they could do because now we have  the threat of not just forking the rook  here uh but you can see the king can't  move to uh d8 here or d7 because of the  rook uh the bishop can't even come block  anything else because of the knights  being uh the one doing the attacking so  king to f8 and then we have rook to d8  that's going to be checkmate so they  definitely have some really awful moves  let's look at the not as awful moves  they could see the the fort coming and  so king castles on the king side okay  well we can just take here on c7 and  then we're going to capture  that rook here on a8 now they could try  some different stuff now let's say  knight to  b6 this is defending the rook here on a8  which we can still capture if we want to  but we can also just play bishop to e3  and that stops everything they were  trying to do because now we can just  take this knight and then there's no way  for them to stop us taking the rook here  on  a 8. from here they could try  knight 2 c6 trying to open things up so  the rook here on f8 can defend that but  then we're just going to take with our  bishop  and then this does open up for the rook  to swing over to b8  but now our bishop can come c5 and yes  we did not capture the rook but we did  get a free piece here on  b6 and now we still have a very very  dominant position we still have a knight  that's putting a lot of pressure on  black we have a very active bishop here  on c5 we can get our light square bishop  uh involved into the game we can also  get our rook involved into the game so  this is going to be a pretty easy end  game for  white now if we come back  let's say instead of castling on the  king side they say okay if you're  attacking the pawn on c7 let's just go  ahead and play king to d8 that stops the  immediate threat here and that is true  but now white has the move pawn e6 and  you can see that's one of the reasons  that i like bishop on f4  is it allows us to attack this bishop or  this pawn here on c7 with our bishop and  our knight see both of those pieces  lined up on this pawn and so how do they  stop it from here okay first they attack  and take with their own pawn  attacking our knight that's fine we were  always going to be taking with our  knight on c7 this is again attacking the  rook on a8  maybe they play pawn  e6 or e5 excuse me attacking the bishop  here on f4 okay that's fine because now  we can bring our knight back to e6  attacking the king check king comes to  e8 now we take the bishop on g7 you can  see we're just gobbling out material the  king is no longer going to be safe the  rest of the game for black king can come  to f7 but then bishop h6 holding down  this knight and white's going to have a  pretty easy game from here on out  looked at the knight coming to d7 but if  they were to play knight to g4 this  would also be a mistake here trying to  get the knight out of harm's way but we  still have the move knight to d5  attacking this pawn on c7 so you know if  they castle on the king side we're just  gonna take here on c7 and easily capture  the rook on a8 the slight difference  that you could see is if they were to  play king d8  trying to hold down this pawn on c7  they've put themselves in a  tough position because we have a very  very powerful discovered check because  after the knight moves anywhere then  we're checking our opponent's king and  then we have free reign to just gobble  up some pieces with you know the most  obvious being knight 2 f6 this is check  king is under attack so maybe knight d7  blocking that but then we can just swing  down and take this knight on  g4  so that is the way that i like to attack  the perk defense really some easy wins  that you'll see in this  so remember after you have your normal  setup instead of playing your f4 or  knight to f3  some typical ways that you may play  against the perk defense try out bishop  here to f4 and after they play bishop g7  push forward e5 and just see how many  times they fall for the trap i think  it's going to be more than you would  expect so hopefully you guys enjoyed the  video excited to be making a lot more  content for you if you have any  suggestions for videos you want me to  make this year let me know in the  comments below thank you guys and i'll  see you in the next video  you

